cosmo_test
==========

A small uniform-resolution LCDM box for exercising PBHEF under both hydro solvers: 25 Mpc/h,
32^3 gas + 32^3 dark matter, z=99 to z=49, Planck 2018.

Why not sbcluster. Its dark matter is a zoom with 80x-mass coarse particles sharing PartType1 with
the refined ones, so in donor mode 62 per cent of the coupled energy came from coarse particles and
landed in the 5 per cent of gas cells at the edge of the refined region, at 13.6 times the mean
rate. It also goes to NaN under MFV at the second sync point with PBHEF not compiled in at all.
Here the resolution is uniform and every dark matter particle has gas in its kernel, so the donor
energy budget is a clean 1.0 and both solvers get a fair test.

Files
-----
  monofonic.conf      the monofonIC configuration that produced the ICs, kept for provenance
  cosmo_test.params   GIZMO parameters; run_matrix.sh overrides OutputDir per run
  run_matrix.sh       builds and runs the eight configurations
  runs/               everything generated, one directory per configuration

The initial conditions
----------------------
Initial conditions are not kept in the repository, and this is the one problem here whose ICs cannot be
regenerated from a script beside it: they came from monofonIC, which is a separate code. If you have a
copy of scripts/test_problems/ics/cosmo_test32_ics.hdf5 you need nothing else. To rebuild it, run
monofonIC on the monofonic.conf beside this file, set its `filename` to
scripts/test_problems/ics/cosmo_test32_ics.hdf5, and run it from the repository root:

  <path to>/monofonIC scripts/test_problems/outputs/cosmo_test/monofonic.conf

monofonIC omits two header attributes that read_ic.c opens without checking, so add them or every
run prints an HDF5 error stack at startup:

  python3 -c "import h5py,numpy as np; f=h5py.File('scripts/test_problems/ics/cosmo_test32_ics.hdf5','r+'); \
    f['Header'].attrs['NumPart_Total_HighWord']=np.zeros(6,dtype=np.uint32); \
    f['Header'].attrs['Flag_DoublePrecision']=np.int32(0)"

Both values were already being taken as zero, since header is file-scope and therefore zeroed, and
zero is right for a single-precision IC under 2^32 particles. The diagnostics were cosmetic; the
attributes are added so a real value can never be missed.

Change GridRes in monofonic.conf to change resolution; the softening in cosmo_test.params is the
mean interparticle spacing over 30 and has to be changed with it.

Running
-------
From the repository root:

  scripts/test_problems/outputs/cosmo_test/run_matrix.sh 2          # both solvers, the default
  scripts/test_problems/outputs/cosmo_test/run_matrix.sh 2 mfm      # one solver only

The eight configurations are the two hydro solvers crossed with no PBHEF, PBHEF=1 (receiver),
PBHEF=2 (donor, which weights by the solid angle of List et al. eq. 6 by default), and PBHEF=2 with
PBHEF_MASS_WEIGHTS (donor, the mass-weighted kernel of their eq. 5).

What to look for
----------------
  - the donor budget fraction, printed every step, should be 1.000000
  - no NaN in any run
  - MFM and MFV should both reach z=49 in 129 steps
  - the last column of pbhef_energy.txt, the energy the gas received over the analytic total

All eight configurations pass. Measured ratios of injected to analytic energy at z=49:

  MFM donor, either weighting    0.99999999
  MFV donor, either weighting    0.99999999
  MFM receiver                   0.99749600
  MFV receiver                   0.99755300

The MFV runs are the ones that matter for the donor storage convention, which holds a share of the
donor's power rather than a specific energy: gas cells there change mass by up to 11 per cent over the
run, so a specific energy would deliver the wrong amount by that much, and the ratio still reads one.

The budget fraction alone cannot see that, because it accumulates at deposit time and reads one whether
or not the mass handling is right. Only the energy ratio can, since it integrates what was delivered.

Note also that the donors here are active on every step, so this problem does not exercise a receiver
sub-cycling below its donor. The density_jump and gas_poor problems do.
